theory:
## Using panic_if

### Instead of:
```xiom
if ptr == null {
  panic("null pointer in process_data");
}
```

### Write:
```xiom
panic_if(ptr == null, "null pointer in process_data");
```

### Multiple Guards
```xiom
fn connect(host: Str, port: Int) -> Connection {
  panic_if(host.len() == 0, "host must not be empty");
  panic_if(port <= 0, "port must be positive, got: " + port.to_str());
  panic_if(port > 65535, "port must be <= 65535, got: " + port.to_str());
  // Connect...
}
```

### Guard Clauses with panic_if
```xiom
fn process_request(req: &Request) -> Response {
  panic_if(req == null, "request is null");
  panic_if(!req.is_valid(), "invalid request: " + req.error_info());
  panic_if(!req.is_authorized(), "unauthorized request from " + req.user());

  // Main logic — all guards passed
  return handle(req);
}
```

### panic_if vs assert
```xiom
// assert: stripped in release, for debug
assert(x > 0, "x must be positive");

// panic_if: always active, for production
panic_if(x <= 0, "x must be positive");
```

syntax_ref:
panic_if(condition, "message");

// Equivalent to:
if condition { panic("message"); }

